<<~ ahu slot=`#/o-takiage`>>

!! O Takiage

The o-takiage rite of 2026-10-20 moves these complete items here, and every land since moves its own; each item names nothing still owed by that item.

* `T20` ''A login flip mints a second persona root'' (custody). Custody file names carry the git global email: `readLocalOperatorHint` shells it with a 2 s timeout and swallows every error (its 11 catch wrappers never fire), and 13 call sites re-shell. A flip (timeout, unset `HOME`, `GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL`, an edited email) lets `generateOrLoadPersonaGroupRoot` mint a second root with no founding card, orphaning the first; a `found` on a flipped login mints a second key and KEL. The vessel key's own mint stays guarded by the founding-card read-back. The suffix retires and custody resolves its home once at open (#/the-floor; the census already reads every suffix as one custody). Red: mint a root under one email, flip it, assert the next call refuses by name; control: the unchanged config loads. Lane `recut-custody` (the stride notes). Landed, PR #138 (`3726248e5`): fixed custody names inside the one custody home; the operator's re-found notice rides the PR.
* `E1` The ember: the serving tier under its own key, the ember pin, the raise with its count and record, four roads of removal (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/ember`>>). Its three questions stand ruled (#/rulings), and `E2` to `E4` carry what each ruling owes. E.
* `P20` The carriage gates (`carriage-board.ts`, `vessel-raise.ts`, `nexus-carriage.ts`) count at the head alone, while `lares nexus members` honours anchored ancestry, so the list can show a member a gate does not yet honour. Built (PR #50): the carrier folds read the anchored lineage, and the contract writer cites the carried heads it supersedes across epochs for both families, so an uncarry, a roll and a re-carry read in order. Built (PR #54): a gate verifies a place contract carried across a charter roll in either direction, a newer gate walking the arm's roll anchors and an older gate accepting the presenter's charter epochs only where they extend its held lineage and pass `verifySealLineage`, each failing closed without its proof; the presenter assembles both, and `readBoardPresentation` folds with ancestry. The herm-mesh and mesh-scenarios witnesses join after A's boot roll. The held-out cell `herm-mesh-roll` (PR #57) rolls A's charter between two herm joins after the boot serves, each herm naming its own head, and presents the opening carry at the rolled head twice: under A's own roll anchor it verifies, and under the same anchor parts signed outside the seats it falls silent by place-contract name. P.
* `W18` The founding hearth's carriage fold walks the roll anchors (PR #50): after a charter roll, the carrier folds at the carriage pull's refresh, `placeCarriedNexuses`, the membership refold and the carry-bundle take count every act the anchored lineage carries, and count none without an anchor (`carriageAncestryFromBoard`; red and controls in `carrier-fold-across-roll.test.ts`). Held-out herm-mesh and the nexus cell then read A filing all four pins and verifying relay-2, and with `P20`'s gate half (PR #54) both read green. W.
* `W21` A joinee mounts the hearth's `@lararium` corpus read-only beside its own, while the oracle plane never crosses (#/rulings, 2026-10-16). Each vessel's pointers rest in its own `@catalog` (PR #52), so a joined vessel's pointer no longer replaces its hearth's, and `shared-oracle-doc.probe` holds that two synced vessels each keep their own. The floor reads that remain owed stand in `W26`. W.
* `W25` The carried bag event names its creator in the clear, where the WHO plane's veil-born group hides it: measured red (`packages/lararium-keyhive/tests/bag-event-creator.probe.test.ts`); `K15`'s step 2 hides it, since no vessel-wide identity then creates a face's bag (#/rulings, 2026-10-16 and 2026-10-18). W.
* `W27` A write reaches its wiki's working layer (#/rulings, 2026-10-18). Built (PR #55): `LOAD` into a canon bag refuses, naming the promotion it owes, while genesis and founding seed through their own named door; an act aimed at a bag opens the bag's own wiki, its recipe record or the floor, through one mesh record-or-floor export the surface recipe and the node's open and act paths share, and lands in its working slot; a mount that cannot stand refuses by its cause; the daemon wiki keeps the daemon's own reach. `W24` walks it. A wiki act on an unmounted wiki mounts it once the keyholder gate clears the cap, built in PR #134; the gate refuses any other denial with no mount. Owed: a typed bag-not-registered refusal in place of the message match, and timeout cleanup of a mount that never answers (rides porch5). W.
* `W28` The sealed channel's frame layout stands frozen (`gate-exchange.ts` `GATE_FRAME`; kit <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/w28-gate-lane`>> #/the-frozen-frames): six tags in two direction blocks, a reserved-tag rule so a later frame adds a tag and moves none, fixed body and subject bounds and 65-byte finish and verdict frames, witnessed by `gate-frame-freeze.test.ts`. The gate answers an upgrade only after its first flight decrypts (`FaceGate.preflight`), so a flight no bundle opens draws a destroyed socket before any 101. No wire byte moved. Built, PR #96. W.
* `K17` The edge frontier (#/rulings, the edge frontier, 2026-10-20). Red-first: the frontier certificate and its verifier on node and browser alike; the signed empty genesis at every founding; registration of a raise into its signer's next frontier; the widening reader (admit, grant, a content-key seal) acting as of a qualifying frontier and waiting as unknown otherwise; the admit bundle carrying the frontier and its snapshot; the reader persisting its highest accepted head. Reds: a withheld first raise, a deleted local raise, a stale clean frontier, two conflicting frontiers, a missing snapshot, a concurrent raise and lower, an epoch rollover. K. The fleet board's frontier stands built with Wave C. The realm leg, K17-R (kit `docs/pono/k17-realm-lease-lane`, the operator's rulings on forks 3 and 4, 2026-10-22), landed in PR #97 on 2026-10-26: one lease law, so a Nexus or realm lease rises only on a charter quorum's signed advance read under its seal-epoch lineage and the unsigned max-register retires with no reader left; each voucher signs its own hand's frontier; a seal roll signs every anchored realm's head under the new epoch; a split-seat cosign carries its head; and the realm admit bundle carries the board's acts, frontiers and snapshots, so a joiner holding no board acts as of them. The closed roster's window passes to `K18`.
* `I1` The face intent plane (#/rulings, the channel of intent, 2026-10-20; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-intent-plane`>>). Build order, each step red-first: `@intents-<tag>` minted by the PersonaGroup with its entry kinds (intent, outcome, asks and answer, claim, release and takeover, supersede and cancel), each signed by its device's atom and its id a content hash; stand-down and the three verb classes, with a single-effect verb addressed to "any device" refused at issue; the read-side winner; causal voids and the operator's release; the face-join summons moving off the daemon doc onto the plane, which closes two hearths of one face, beside the CLI naming its face per invocation; the daemon wiki's view pane; the rubric's witness (a hundred keystrokes and a save write no entry; one move writes one intent and one outcome); then the far agent's relay seat after its own research. Its reds stand at the carrier's #/the-reds. Its authority reads stand on the face's sealed identity anchor, so it builds on the anchor (operator, 2026-10-20, approving the controller's lean); the relay seat's own research wave follows it. Lead: a Sonnet Episkopos with a Sol and a Copilot seat. I. Built, PR #99, 2026-10-26 (kit `docs/pono/i1-intent-lane`): `@intents-<tag>` stands as the fifth face plane, registered by a signed `plane` act and never projected to disk; each entry signs under its own domain over the signed-act image, the intent id hashing issuer, verb, arguments and nonce; a device stands down on an answered slot, a single-effect verb addressed to a class or "any device" refuses at issue and an unpinned one waits on an `asks` gate whose `answer` names the atom; the winner reads by preference, then the lowest `H(atom ‖ intent id)`, with every duplicate kept visible; claims, takeover and causal voids read no clock, a finite `every` names a roster act on `@persona` and waits while its reader lacks it, and the persona root, the face's operator key, signs the release that outranks a claim; the face-join summons rides the face's plane (`lares intent issue|show|ask|answer|claim|takeover|release|cancel|supersede --face N`), the daemon-doc verb retired, and the effect door fences the pinned hearth and the operation id before any re-key, so a second hearth refuses and a replay returns its grant; the daemon wiki shows intents through a read-only pane; the rubric witness reads a seeded duplicate before trusting its zero. The far agent's relay seat passes to `I2`.
* `W14` The floor card a banner carries reads signed: built, PR #100, 2026-10-26 (kit `docs/pono/w14-floor-pin-lane`, with `W15` and `W16`). The card carries a place floor pin claim and that place's KEL events, and it never anchors itself: a reader holds the place AID apart (a `#anchor=<aid>` link kept per origin, the banner's own `floor anchor:` line, or the first claim filed over an authenticated crossing), and `readPlaceFloorPin` resolves the claim's signer at its named head through `verifyPlaceDutyAtHead`, folding that head back to the inception. An unanchored card refuses, and an offline browser read names its floor "as of my last sync". W.
* `W15` A floor pin claim carries its place's KEL head, and the pin index folds claims as a set, so arrival order selects nothing: a descendant head replaces, a known ancestor refuses as a rollback and stays as ancestry evidence, and an equal-head divergence, a sibling rotation, a second AID at one key or a noted KEL fork surfaces as a fork while the index serves the settled ancestor or nothing. A rotated floor key re-files live under its new signer's name; unclaimed pins keep first-wins. Board reads and the index stay bounded (512 claim and 4096 KEL events per read, 256 claims per set, 1024 noted forks). Built, PR #100. W.
* `W16` Two-vault carry lands its floor pin: the carry partial carries entry, claim and KEL; a counted carry lands act, claim and KEL entries in one change; the carrier seal verifies at its signer's head (`verifyCarrierContractAtHead`); and a herm relays the claim with its KEL events, so a cofounded Nexus carries its herm. A rotated place carries again under its new signer, and readers serve the older head until that carry counts. Its witness, a four-head rotation-and-carry storm under twelve shuffled orders with restarts and offline gaps on node and browser alike, ends every order on one head and accepts no stale pin. Built, PR #100. W.
* `G15` The refold's answers: built, PR #101, 2026-10-26 (kit `docs/pono/g15-refold-lane`). The after-roll chain stands as its own module (`after-roll-chain.ts`, `createAfterRoll`) that a vector runs, so `after-roll-awaited.test.ts` reads behaviour, not source shape. Its bound reads causal: passes run one at a time, each holder owns at most one queued pass, a roll landing before its holder's pass starts folds into it and one landing mid-pass queues that holder's next pass at the tail, so a storm over H holders runs at most H+1 passes, round-robin, with no overlap and no starved holder, and a throwing clock spy proves no wall clock decides. A stage that never settles still holds the passes behind it, since no timer may cut it. The plugin build's transcript records the logical repo path of the TW5 binary it actually ran (`tw5-bin-path.ts`), and a build that read another binary reads a mismatch. A labelled re-bake carries the declared bundle and re-takes the floor snapshots. G.
* `G38` One stream-manifest shape stands across both languages: the Python sensorium writer (`sensorium.py`, `_write_stream_manifest_unlocked` and `set_stream_ephemeral`) emits each stream manifest's `lifecycle` field, and the reader (`packages/lararium-node/src/sensorium.ts`) then requires it, so the derivation fallback from `ephemeral` and `halfLife` burns. Built, PR #104. G.
* `H7` One doctrine line still calls an introduction's lifetime unruled (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/membership-doctrine#/the-invite`>>). The porch read #/the-invite (PR #104): the answer consumes the introduction, and the dials count and read no clock, so no doctrine line calls its lifetime unruled. H, canon.
* `G17` Tests only: the node door's scratch marker. Built (PR #105): a marked root names its TEST SCRATCH notice, an unmarked root names none, and the seated holder stays locked after the notice, so the marker never opens custody (harbor: every boot rests at the floor). The closing review that named mutation survivors rests in neither the tree nor the log (porch, PR #104), so that clause retired. G.
* `A9` The browser harness keeps no repository bind: the `qa` runner builds the `browser-harness` Dockerfile target, which bakes the pinned Playwright client, the two walks, their join helper and the custody probe (PR #105); `harness-image-law` scans it with the old bind as its red control, and the mesh's two browser peers stay its named exceptions. B.
* `W20` Burned names outside the wire's files still guarded by name. The porch burned every such guard outside `K15`'s held files (PR #102, kit `docs/pono/porch-lane`): the browser's burned-store absence check, and the retired verbs, flags and exports in the CLI, vault, nexus and cabal tests. The CLI's unknown-command refusal reads generic, with a nonsense control. `K15`'s bonds burned the last, a retired cabal name in `cap-event-variant-honest.test.ts` (PR #106). W.
* `F8` The control-event leg stands built: `lar:control-event` and `lar:control-result` replace `wiki:dom-event` and `wiki:dom-input` (`lar-camera.ts`, `projection-sink.ts`). The porch burned the guard in `lar-wire.test.ts` (PR #102); D2 burned the last, in `projection-input-leg.test.ts` (PR #103). O1.
* `F4` Node callers move to sessions; receipts bind to the face; verb outcomes and the effect index follow the working layer with their reader (`verb-vm.ts`, `uds-channel.ts`). O1. Built in PR #108 (kit `docs/pono/hands-spine-lane`): every store-door record and sealed act that acts as a face rides a session of that face, a record naming another face refuses `face-row-other-face`, and the receipt binds the face's proof subject.
* `F5` The durability receipt, rung 2's node half. O1. Built in PR #108 (kit `docs/pono/hands-spine-lane`): a durable outcome rests under the face's proof subject and the input digest, and a later session of the same face reads it.
* `F9` The switch; the vessel-wide selector retires. Its one persisted shape, the selector's two stores (the node's active-persona index and the browser's `ACTIVE_PERSONA_STORE`), retired with `K15` Wave D (PR #95): the worn face lives as session state on node and browser, and no store keeps it. The switch act itself lands additive. O1. The switch stands built in PR #108 (kit `docs/pono/hands-spine-lane`): `session:switch` runs a five-step fence that moves one session alone, and `lares persona wear` retired with the vessel-wide selector; a command names its face (`--face N`) or reads the one face standing open.
* `A8` `persona wear` on a standing vessel waits on the session switch. O1. Closed in PR #108: `persona wear` retired; a session switches in place.
* `W17` Face switching on one device, and a second joined face on one vessel. House. W. Built in PR #108 (kit `docs/pono/hands-spine-lane`): each joined face opens its own session and switches in place; the daemon judging a second face's atom as that face rides `K19`.
* `K19` One Keyhive provider per joined face at the daemon (operator ruling, 2026-10-27; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract#/the-hands`>>). Built, PRs #110, #118, #121 and #127 (kit `docs/pono/k19-hive-lane`): each unlocked joined face boots its own provider over its own event-store scope and routes by the presenter's atom key; the content fence denies by default; content cannot claim the channel; a session acts only as the face it wears; and the realm feed binds to the face→leaf bond. What stays owed rests in `K20` and `K21`, and a council's quorum Handle stays with `K16`. K.
* `I2` The far agent's relay seat: the least relay trust and attestation that stops capability escalation and argument substitution while keeping causal provenance. Built, PRs #112 and #125 (kit `docs/pono/i2-relay-lane`): delegation and revoke ride the plane, admission leaves the entry unchanged and rides beside it as a receipt, both composers register a face's planes only as its signed log names them, a malformed sibling record leaves only its own face unread, and the SharedWorker holder listens before it loads. What stays owed rests in `I3`. I.
* `T1` The Stryker probe (measured 2026-10-31; its configs and root devDeps rest at the tag `archive/stryker-probe`, `5240e7de6`): StrykerJS 10.1.1 with its vitest runner activates and kills mutants under vitest 5.0.3, picking the `>` test-name separator, so the earlier no-mutant reading retires. On `lares-cli` (`kinds.ts`, `engine.ts`) 994 mutants score 72.7% in 812 s at concurrency 4 (876 MB sandbox, 690 MB peak); an unchanged incremental rerun takes 31 s; a `lararium-mesh` slice (`cap-compose.ts`, `quorum-entry.ts`) scores 86.1% in 16 s. The suites kill a Stryker mutant on all 14 hand-catalog lines; Stryker reproduces about 9 of their shapes and cannot generate the operand swap, the statement insertion or the template reorder, so the catalog keeps its job. Recipe: run from the repo root, `inPlace` false, the runner's `pool` set to `forks` (its own default `threads` overrides the suites'), the sandbox lacking gitignored `dist/` and untracked files, any failing dry-run test aborting the run. `lararium-node` stays unprobed. T.
* `L5` Cold start reaches the genesis seed (walk gap G5). A founding reads `LAR_GENESIS`, else `<LAR_ROOT>/genesis` (`found.ts`), and nothing stages it, so a cold root takes a hand copy. Built, PR #143 (`ec3c990be`): one genesis resolver serves the daemon, `main.ts` and the CLI, so a cold root reaches the build's shipped genesis with no hand copy while an explicit `LAR_GENESIS` still wins, and a shipped-seed founding pins its seed CID, the pin moving only on a successful founding. What stays owed rests in `T16` (the daemon's no-argument readers) and the porch (the pin's crash window). L.
* `T19` ''Carried posture goes stale'' (security, public shelf, strangers only). A refold drops no-longer-held sockets synchronously and then fires the gate re-sweep un-awaited, so the drop always sorts on the previous sweep's posture: a carried Nexus tightening from open to private keeps seated strangers until a later refold, and a knock in the gap sorts stale. The realm-plane refold drops with no re-sweep at all; `unstand()` (`face-gate-server.ts`) closes no seated socket; sweeps run unserialized, so an older read can land last and re-stand a gate, while the intent relay serializes and shuts every gate on a read failure. Cure: refresh the readings, then drop, as one owned step on both refold paths; serialize the sweeps; shut on a read failure. Red: tighten a carried charter with a stranger seated, refold, assert the socket closes in that refold; controls: an own-Nexus flip, the herm staying open. Lane `recut-gates`, both slices (the stride notes). Landed, PR #141 (`5535ebe8c`): one owner for a vessel's gate sweep (`gateSweep`, a transport-free mesh stage) refreshes the carried postures, drops what they demote, then moves the gates, on both refold paths; a gate stood down cuts the sockets it seated. What stays owed rests in the porch (the composer's cell order, the rig's notes).
* `T22` ''Share inputs withhold until they stand'' (defense in depth). `kahuProtected`, `antigenRing` and `edgeShadows` allow while null; only the build order (`T6`) keeps a peer from arriving first, and Automerge's share policy allows everything by default, so the house guard stands as the only deny layer. Each null input withholds by name. Also the floor-on-a-stale-store case: a floor booting on a store left by an earlier sovereign run reads the old Kahu documents unclassed; red: the share verdict with empty protected ids, a dialed peer and the Kahu canon id must deny. On the lane that red read green (a locked floor's holder reads incomplete, and the guard withholds all), so the clause retires at the lane's land. Lane `recut-gates`, slice 1. Landed, PR #141: a share input that has not stood withholds by name from wire and sibling peers, and every keel names its system canon.
* `T27` ''Teardown seams''. A refused boot leaves a stale cas door; the sync gates carry no torn-down guard, so a sweep in flight during teardown can register a route that makes the next composition throw "already owned" and stand no gates until restart; the custody and dyad doors drop before the daemon island shuts. Lane `recut-gates`: the refused boot's door in slice 1, the rest in slice 2. Landed, PR #141: the island's doors drop after the island shuts, and a refused boot leaves no door answering. What stays owed rests in the porch (the rig's fixed settle window).
* `T33` ''A planted roll mark skips a roll'' (security, MEDIUM, predates `U1`; the train t2 cold read, wave three). `raisesMarkedAnswered` trusts any `rolled/<raise>` ledger entry and `markRaiseRolled` stores no receipt; `ledger:edge` reads as synced (`operator-daemon-behavior.ts`) and the vessel-private class does not list it, so a planted mark makes seat-duties skip a removal roll and, beside `U1`'s durable owed list, clears that list. Cure: a mark counts only beside a kept roll whose receipt this vessel's key signed, and the ledger joins the vessel-private class. Red: plant a `rolled/<raise>` entry from another device, remove a reader, and the roll still runs and the owed list keeps the raise; control: a vessel's own signed roll clears it. Landed, PR #144 (`13d24a218`): one entry predicate shared by every reader and the writer counts a mark only under a receipt this vessel's own key signed, and `ledger:edge` crosses to no peer (vessel-private, a `device` owner no relation opens), classed for every face whose key the vessel holds at boot. What stays owed rests in the porch (faces whose keys open after boot).
* `T34` ''A joinee's own foundings never roll'' (security; the same read). A joinee runs no seat-duties, so a bag it founded never rolls its content key when a device leaves the fleet. Owed: the founding vessel rolls its own foundings on a counted removal, whatever its seat. Red: a joinee founds a bag, a device is removed, and a seal after the removal opens to no removed key; control: the seat holder's foundings roll as today. Landed, PR #144: the worn face decides the fleet, never the roster, so a joinee rolls its own foundings.
* `T35` ''A founder's first removal survives a restart unread'' (security, LOW-MEDIUM, consequence measured; the train t2 cold read, wave four, after `U1`'s `N1` cure). A founder's FIRST removal stays owed, the island restarts, and the fold reads unread (the worn fleet root null, or the root not served): the never-rolled gate passes and the removed device opens the post-restart seal. Cure: `fleetRaisesOf` answers `read:false` only for a vessel holding no root for the worn face (a joinee); a vessel with a roster but a null or unmatched worn fleet root throws, so the seal refuses. Red: that founder case refuses; control: a joinee with no roster seals. Takes the next free slot. Landed, PR #144: a worn face whose root or atom stands unread refuses to seal; only a vessel wearing no face reads unread.
* `T3` `LandedLedger.whenLanded`: an event replaces the wiki-shore 2 s poll. Red-first: a landing deferred past the old poll reds the poll form; a title that never lands times out with a named refusal. Clock stays injected. Files: `packages/lararium-tw5/src/landed-ledger.ts`, the `wiki-shore-*` tests. Landed, PR #142 (`86811edc5`): `LandedLedger.whenLanded` replaces the wiki-shore poll loops.
* `U1` The signer hand-over into the island with its chain check (the keystone, #/the-trains, O1). Built, PR #140 (`b333ed731`): a face's session seals into and reopens from the sealed store through a sign door, so no session key crosses into the island; only Nexus-anchored sessions seal, another face neither seals, opens nor reads, and the `sealed-*` verbs gate on the invocation's face; `holdsWrite` accepts only certificates under the vessel's own chain; a session act settles the bag's owed removal rolls before any seal, and the owed raises outlive a restart in the vessel's own fold. What stays owed rests in `U2` (the booking's nonce), and `K21` (sibling devices' seals). O1.
* ''The recovery-contact rename'' (#/rulings, 2026-10-31, recovery contacts, custodians and the Handle): built in PR #135. The `K18` recovery-key signer reads as a recovery contact across canon, code and the CLI, the seed-share holder as a custodian, and person-facing text says "your Handle" where recovery restores it; guardian keeps its human sense alone. What stays owed rests in `K18`. K.
* `L3` The MCP seat binds the hearth (walk gap G4). The seat's entry in the operator's home config named a deleted worktree copy of `lares_mcp.py` and carried no `LAR_ROOT`, and `~/.venv` held an `mcp` older than its pin, so the seat failed before it spoke. Landed, PR #148 (`1ef844c60`): `resolveLaresMcp(clone)` names the hearth's root (`LAR_ROOT`) in both the native and the surface-container forms and aims at the main clone, and every seat's observe compares env; the hearth owns its surface env (`<hearth>/lares-surface.env`), which the resolver and both container shims read, one layout with no fallback; a missing seat names its own form's cause; the seat's launch refuses by name on an `mcp` below its pin (2.3.0, welded to `requirements.txt` and the image lock); the e2e walk `mcp-seat-binds-the-hearth` reads `meme_put` with `recipe=default` and `lares meme get --recipe default` as the same bytes, and the e2e shards stand the holder venv. The operator's `~/.venv` took `mcp` 2.3.0 by hand (2026-11-01). Its tail, the operator's hand alone: `lares home tend` from the main clone re-writes the home entry, and a lane never runs it (#/cold-start). What stays owed rests in the porch (the holder venv's cache key); the container shim hands compose the hearth's env file or `/dev/null`, so the checkout's `.env` never loads. L.
* `Q` ''The kupono grammar cut'' (#/rulings, 2026-11-01; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/grammar-watch#/the-kupono-cut`>>). Landed, PR #149 (`1e849cfba`), one lane merged once: every sigil value rides named in backticks; the bare arrow left the sigil, and the bearing pair `from=` and `to=` carries the relation; no `@` follows whitespace, and a phase glyph fuses onto its φ; the turn frame folds into one sigil, `set lares=` with `aim`, `steer` and `yield`, a steer carrying no `from`; the ward rides unprefixed; `wai` heads the wikispace conditional; `kahea` takes `name=`, `params=` and `mask=`. The boot seed reorganized through three blind cold readers and a Sonnet and Haiku dogfood wake, which spoke the new grammar every turn, and the operator approved its bytes, the turn frame provisionally. Readers and writers flipped; the plane names live in one constant; the turn harvesters burned whole (mesh turn and bearing harvest, the patch builder, the recall stamp filters, the tw5 bearing reader, the move skeleton, the self-read harvester, both VM harvesters, bare `lares sense pour <path>`, `form_encoder.py`, `loci_io.py`, `tools/turn-parity.mjs`); `docs/relational-parameter` burned by o-takiage, its parser trace now in `memetic-wikitext#/lexical-structure`; the `file-path` meta field retired (residency rides the envelope; 773 carriers stripped); the recall verb refuses a burned or search-only filter by name; the cut re-cut all 790 carriers idempotent and re-sealed no broken seal; genesis re-baked once. The VSCode-TW5-Syntax pointer followed to its verified glass head (`0440b57dc`). What stays owed rests in `G47` (the three sensorium placeholders), `G48` (a rewound turn stays recallable), `G49` (recall arguments that drop unread), `G50` (a paired `wai` swallows its body), `G51` (`lar:` fragments inside tick values), the legacy-check pyre (#/side-lanes) and train ''R'' (#/the-trains). Q.
* `R` ''Block and inline sigils at TiddlyWiki's parsing parity'' (#/rulings, 2026-11-01). Landed, PR #150 (`fe0bbb8bd`), one lane merged once: the block check rides the ETX mark's named `bcc` value, ``<<^ code=`&#x0003;` bcc=`ni:///sha-256;…`>>``, its digest read over the span with that one run deleted (RFC 6376 §3.7's emptied `b=`), and every other ETX spelling tears, the retired trailer by name; the cut's sixth pass moved 791 feet byte for byte, verify-first, and minted no seal; a block sigil counts only when its `>>` ends the line, else its paragraph holds it inline as TiddlyWiki splits it, and the graceful-floor diagnostic fires only for the block-only heads (`lar-flow: block`: the five frame marks, `ahu` and its fragment mirror); every reader agrees inside the sigil space (the frame package, the plugin, tree-sitter, `scanAhu`, the weave); every boot-seed fence names its grammar (`memetic-wikitext`, `toml meta`, `text`), and every fence across seed and corpus outruns its inner ticks; a whole-tree seal census names every stale or torn seal, zero undeclared beside its known positive. The reads' cures rode the lane: the ahu line law (a closer counts only when it opens its line or shares its opener's line, in `scanAhu` and the plugin alike, its parity witness proving itself on a planted mutant); tilde fences retired at TiddlyWiki's codeblock law, so a tilde run fences nothing; `verifyBcc` reads each frame fault once, as torn; the weave keeps the text the seal covers, reading a frame mark only alone on its line; and the seed step burned its 40K budget clause beside its kept missing-seed check (operator: //"We can exceed the 40K ceiling if needed, as we now deterministically load the boot seed via hooks."//). A blind Opus read shipped it, the operator approved the seed (//"this reads pono"//), and genesis carries its closing bake. The VSCode-TW5-Syntax pointer followed to `6dd602cfd` (`cc7954d46`). What stays owed rests in `G52` (the cold-read LOWs), `G53` (a weave pin's stored check), the legacy-check pyre (the `retired-trailer` refusal burns there) and ''harden'' (the head the seal leaves outside, #/side-lanes). R.
* ''The deps before the founding'' (#/rulings, 2026-11-01). Landed, PR #151 (`3fa7bfc06`), one commit per family over a regenerated lockfile, every pin exact: `@automerge/automerge` 3.5.0 on Trial A's green (every suite equal to its control, the e2e sets 201 of 201, genesis unmoved); the `vitest` family 5.0.5, which cures the dev-server `server.fs.deny` advisory; `ws` 8.22.1; `playwright` 1.64.1 with its image tag; `@types/node` 26.6.6; `esbuild` 0.28.2; `acorn` 8.19.0; `web-tree-sitter` and `tree-sitter-cli` 0.27.1 together, with the grammar's artifact pins. A blind Opus supply-chain read found the chain clean (47 of 47 integrity checks, no new names, scripts or publishers) and held the land until the artifact pins moved. Trial B read red, so `@keyhive/keyhive` holds 0.3.0-alpha.1 through the founding (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/keyhive-zero-six-port`>>). What stays owed rests in ''dropdown'' (the one regression the land left on the span: the web-tw5 search-dropdown e2e reads red twice at `3fa7bfc06`, green before it at `cc7954d46`), the deps' tail (#/side-lanes) and ''harden'' (the phantom-record cure the supply read found). G.

